{"versions":[{"path":"versions/e8f218cc-e868-4045-8ac6-729b6823831e/35c18d03046357585eb6c89a5e57dfee04e3335f787abbe2f5fe49e8d1cbcb86.json","value":{"schema_version":1,"revision_scheme":"holly-question-v1","bank_id":"3eb3811b-4425-46ad-8bb6-a0504dca151b","question_id":"e8f218cc-e868-4045-8ac6-729b6823831e","revision":"sha256:35c18d03046357585eb6c89a5e57dfee04e3335f787abbe2f5fe49e8d1cbcb86","kind":"mcqs","content":{"question":[{"type":"text","content":"Insert 2, 1, 4, 5, 9, 3, 6, 7 into an initially empty AVL tree.  Which one of the following statements is FALSE?"}],"options":[{"type":"text","content":"4 is the root","correct":false,"option_id":"fb5a9314-0618-4de9-bea7-619555fb9a0f"},{"type":"text","content":"3 and 7 are siblings","correct":true,"option_id":"dc870aa3-c881-4ff7-9135-92202203acd6"},{"type":"text","content":"2 and 6 are siblings","correct":false,"option_id":"232e60c8-b7e7-460d-9c7c-debc29779c6f"},{"type":"text","content":"9 is the parent of 7","correct":false,"option_id":"d1ab5661-5112-443e-b752-5957fc26824e"}]}}},{"path":"versions/f6b01fd3-36d1-4f21-bd9b-5f1bb29fdcd3/4b0bb28eaf2ba670dda244521fb05d0eef6253fe78ca67470374d3de091c5b08.json","value":{"schema_version":1,"revision_scheme":"holly-question-v1","bank_id":"3eb3811b-4425-46ad-8bb6-a0504dca151b","question_id":"f6b01fd3-36d1-4f21-bd9b-5f1bb29fdcd3","revision":"sha256:4b0bb28eaf2ba670dda244521fb05d0eef6253fe78ca67470374d3de091c5b08","kind":"mcqs","content":{"question":[{"type":"text","content":"If the depth of an AVL tree is 6 (the depth of an empty tree is defined \nto be -1), then the minimum possible number of nodes in this tree is:"}],"options":[{"type":"text","content":"13","correct":false,"option_id":"f5743f8b-db59-4410-9d54-ff878485019f"},{"type":"text","content":"17","correct":false,"option_id":"5d2f3cf1-6f73-4555-8bcd-6f4b3c4e95e6"},{"type":"text","content":"20","correct":false,"option_id":"8d36a182-d777-49c7-8148-f35f141a55b5"},{"type":"text","content":"33","correct":true,"option_id":"cd248663-2300-4d9c-85c4-2a13b5a6604c"}]}}},{"path":"versions/acdaf13b-ba8b-425a-84c9-3e31575da453/8831ccbbbc4c818646f2122b2046be15824be1315beae1ecca533e87b68654fc.json","value":{"schema_version":1,"revision_scheme":"holly-question-v1","bank_id":"3eb3811b-4425-46ad-8bb6-a0504dca151b","question_id":"acdaf13b-ba8b-425a-84c9-3e31575da453","revision":"sha256:8831ccbbbc4c818646f2122b2046be15824be1315beae1ecca533e87b68654fc","kind":"mcqs","content":{"question":[{"type":"text","content":"For the result of accessing the keys 3, 9, 1, 5 in order in the splay \ntree in the following figure, which one of the following statements is \nFALSE?"},{"type":"image","src":"../../assets/9d0625275752d91e7496d35564b4c8519554965a5fadcabac83a7cf99261ad67.jpg"}],"options":[{"type":"text","content":"5 is the root","correct":false,"option_id":"51c35e5e-277c-4e66-acbd-40bb31fe2051"},{"type":"text","content":"1 and 9 are siblings","correct":false,"option_id":"0a53fd28-97d3-4104-9cf0-e31bd96da184"},{"type":"text","content":"6 and 10 are siblings","correct":false,"option_id":"dd158321-2fed-49b4-a76f-f10445a7e0fe"},{"type":"text","content":"3 is the parent of 4","correct":true,"option_id":"09eddfb5-383c-4bff-9aca-0edf26656cac"}]}}},{"path":"versions/c11c00f4-9279-4e7d-bfea-19c17cea63b4/b896759686d6713e9771448e5a482f59bd14737df05bbcf9d23650b8b8ba16cd.json","value":{"schema_version":1,"revision_scheme":"holly-question-v1","bank_id":"3eb3811b-4425-46ad-8bb6-a0504dca151b","question_id":"c11c00f4-9279-4e7d-bfea-19c17cea63b4","revision":"sha256:b896759686d6713e9771448e5a482f59bd14737df05bbcf9d23650b8b8ba16cd","kind":"mcqs","content":{"question":[{"type":"text","content":"Finding the maximum key from a splay tree will result in a tree with its root having no right subtree."}],"options":[{"type":"text","content":"True","correct":true,"option_id":"37342509-bc77-4414-8daa-4704aa071399"},{"type":"text","content":"False","correct":false,"option_id":"132604fa-5d94-46ad-9525-415d64f31b27"}]}}},{"path":"versions/033fab3c-dbcd-4a6e-b8bc-bb1f9e310792/d4458beae320d145e79f229f87c5effdca385d457b640f8e35c5618502b424c1.json","value":{"schema_version":3,"revision_scheme":"holly-question-v1","bank_id":"3eb3811b-4425-46ad-8bb6-a0504dca151b","question_id":"033fab3c-dbcd-4a6e-b8bc-bb1f9e310792","revision":"sha256:d4458beae320d145e79f229f87c5effdca385d457b640f8e35c5618502b424c1","kind":"mcqs","content":{"question":[{"type":"text","content":"Consider the following buffer management problem. Initially the buffer size (the number of blocks) is one. Each block can accommodate exactly one item. As soon as a new item arrives, check if there is an available block. If yes, put the item into the block, induced a cost of one. Otherwise, the buffer size is doubled, and then the item is able to put into. Moreover, the old items have to be moved into the new buffer so it costs $k+1$ to make this insertion, where $k$ is the number of old items. Clearly, if there are $N$ items, the worst-case cost for one insertion can be $\\Omega (N)$. To show that the average cost is $O(1)$, let us turn to the amortized analysis. To simplify the problem, assume that the buffer is full after all the $N$ items are placed. Which of the following potential functions works?"}],"options":[{"type":"text","content":"The number of items currently in the buffer","correct":false,"option_id":"00532716-f96e-4175-a515-de4a399a80c9"},{"type":"text","content":"The opposite number of items currently in the buffer","correct":false,"option_id":"84fc0ebd-00b7-4edb-8aa2-ffefd75c55d3"},{"type":"text","content":"The number of available blocks currently in the buffer","correct":false,"option_id":"8eb2090b-e8da-4f65-8087-ec1b0295024d"},{"type":"text","content":"The opposite number of available blocks in the buffer","correct":false,"option_id":"830c3022-ae11-48f9-96a0-59bd0c14ad47"}],"answer_status":"unavailable","selection_mode":"single"}}},{"path":"versions/f96783a3-3443-4664-9ae7-d7251270d258/e4fc408ffb6867036bff7675803f1ff11a3ac391d06ee52b621f90645b51237f.json","value":{"schema_version":3,"revision_scheme":"holly-question-v1","bank_id":"3eb3811b-4425-46ad-8bb6-a0504dca151b","question_id":"f96783a3-3443-4664-9ae7-d7251270d258","revision":"sha256:e4fc408ffb6867036bff7675803f1ff11a3ac391d06ee52b621f90645b51237f","kind":"mcqs","content":{"question":[{"type":"text","content":"For one operation, if its amortized time bound is $O(\\log N)$, then its worst-case time bound must be $O(\\log N)$."}],"options":[{"type":"text","content":"True","correct":false,"option_id":"9f35e261-e4b3-4989-a06a-1da1e6704bdd"},{"type":"text","content":"False","correct":false,"option_id":"8fdf0448-ca5b-404b-9b2d-5dcb910fa85c"}],"answer_status":"unavailable","selection_mode":"single"}}}]}